code.code:
// main.ts — the app's entry point
import * as Sentry from "@sentry/browser";
import { mountApp } from "./app";

// 'Deferred so the SDK never competes with first paint.'
setTimeout(() => {
  Sentry.init({ dsn: import.meta.env.VITE_SENTRY_DSN });
}, 0);

// mountApp renders the cart island synchronously and reads the cart
// from localStorage during that first render.
mountApp(document.getElementById("root")!);

// QA verification, run against the deployed build:
// 1. open the site
// 2. run  throw new Error("observability-test")  in the DevTools console
// 3. nothing appears in the project
//
// Ticket conclusion: 'SDK broken in production. The checkout TypeError
// was probably a browser extension.'

scenarios.modelAnswer:
Three defects, in order of blast radius.

1. The init is a scheduled task, so it runs after the current script finishes — and `mountApp` finishes first. The cart island renders synchronously and reads localStorage during that render, which is exactly where Friday's TypeError was thrown: at that moment no Sentry client exists and no global `error`/`unhandledrejection` handler is installed, so the throw is lost. Be precise about the boundary: what is lost is module evaluation plus the synchronous first render — the failing path — while a rejection that settles after the deferred callback may still be captured. That is why the failure looks intermittent and why 'nothing arrives' is a misleading signal.

2. There is no `environment` and no `release`, so the one issue that did arrive cannot be tied to the Friday deploy or separated from preview traffic. The same gap also makes the ticket's premise unverifiable: you cannot compare this release against its predecessor, because the events never carried a release identity.

3. The verification is invalid. An error typed into the DevTools console is evaluated in the console's own context and never reaches the `window` handlers the SDK hooks, so it exercises no handler, no breadcrumb buffer, no transport and no tagging. Nothing arriving proves nothing; and had it arrived, it would still not have tested the path that mattered.

Also worth naming: 'deferred so the SDK never competes with first paint' is a real cost concern, but the answer is not to postpone error capture — it is to init early with the cheapest configuration (errors first, tracing sampled or lazy-loaded) and keep the heavy signals off the critical path.

Corrected entry point:

```ts
import * as Sentry from "@sentry/browser";
import { mountApp } from "./app";

Sentry.init({
  dsn: import.meta.env.VITE_SENTRY_DSN,
  environment: import.meta.env.MODE,       // production | preview | ...
  release: import.meta.env.VITE_RELEASE,   // commit SHA served by this build
  tracesSampleRate: 0.1,                   // sampled, never 1.0 by default
});

// Earlier than any code that can fail, including the first synchronous render.
mountApp(document.getElementById("root")!);

// Verification through an application path, not the console.
const params = new URLSearchParams(location.search);
if (import.meta.env.MODE !== "production" && params.has("verify-observability")) {
  throw new Error("observability-verify");
}
```

Two different claims are being proven, and they need two checks. The pipeline: open that issue and confirm four things — it exists, `environment` and `release` are what this build actually shipped, the stack is symbolicated back to the original component, and the breadcrumbs show the navigation. The ordering: a non-production smoke run that forces the cart render to throw, asserting the issue arrives — same code path as Friday, which is exactly what an app-entry throw cannot demonstrate. If production verification is required, gate it behind a labeled button or flag and filter that label out of alerts so it never becomes incident noise.

code.code:
import { onCLS, onINP, onLCP } from "web-vitals";

const values: number[] = [];

onCLS((metric) => values.push(metric.value));
onINP((metric) => values.push(metric.value));
onLCP((metric) => values.push(metric.value));

window.addEventListener("pagehide", () => {
  const average = values.reduce((sum, v) => sum + v, 0) / values.length;
  navigator.sendBeacon("/analytics", JSON.stringify({ average }));
});

scenarios.modelAnswer:
Four problems, one of them arithmetic.

1. Mixed units in one array. CLS is a unitless score (~0 to 0.25), LCP and INP are milliseconds on very different scales. Their mean has no unit and no meaning, so 'average INP 130 ms' cannot be produced by this code at all — whatever the dashboard shows was aggregated somewhere else, or is this number under a label that does not describe it.

2. The mean is the wrong statistic even per metric. The passing view is the 75th percentile of field data per metric against 2.5 s / 200 ms / 0.1, because Core Web Vitals describe the share of visits with a good experience. An average of 130 ms coexists happily with a quarter of sessions being poor — which is exactly the 'freezes when you tap a size' complaint: INP is interaction-driven, so the users who tap are the ones paying.

3. Identity and segmentation are destroyed. One blob per pageview with a single `average` key drops the metric name, the metric's `id` and `rating`, the route, the release. And a p75 cannot be recovered from per-pageview means: averaging averages is not averaging samples, so no downstream computation can rescue the raw distribution.

4. Edge cases that bias the sample. If the page unloads before any callback fires, `values.length` is 0 and the beacon posts `{"average":null}` (NaN serialises to null), polluting any downstream average. And a visit with no interaction produces no INP at all, so the collected INP sample over-represents low-interaction pages — the opposite of the product page support is complaining about.

Rewrite — one event per metric, raw value, segmentation kept:

```ts
import { onCLS, onINP, onLCP, type Metric } from "web-vitals";

function report(metric: Metric): void {
  const payload = JSON.stringify({
    name: metric.name,                 // 'LCP' | 'INP' | 'CLS'
    value: metric.value,               // raw sample, never pre-aggregated
    rating: metric.rating,             // good | needs-improvement | poor
    id: metric.id,                     // dedupes repeat beacons for one value
    route: location.pathname,
    release: import.meta.env.VITE_RELEASE,
    device: matchMedia("(max-width: 640px)").matches ? "mobile" : "desktop",
  });
  navigator.sendBeacon("/rum/vitals", payload);  // survives unload
}

onLCP(report);
onINP(report);
onCLS(report);
```

Server-side: store the raw samples and compute p75 per metric per segment (route × device × release) over a window, plus the share of visits above the threshold, which is the number that maps to 'how many users had a bad experience'. Keep `sendBeacon` on `pagehide` — the transport is the part that already worked; what changed is the payload and the aggregation.

code.code:
export async function checkout(user: User, cart: Cart): Promise<Order> {
  console.log("checkout start", user);
  try {
    const order = await createOrder(user, cart);
    console.log("checkout ok", user.email, order);
    return order;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("checkout failed", error, user, cart);
    return failedOrder();
  }
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
Four defects, and the last one is not about logging at all.

- Whole-object dumps. `user`, `cart` and `order` are serialised as they come: no schema you can filter on, no way to aggregate, and whatever those objects gain tomorrow ships to the log backend (including the token someone will eventually add to `user`).
- Personal data by design. `user.email` is written explicitly, and the cart and order objects carry addresses and item details. Redaction belongs before transport, on the client: a server-side filter cannot un-send what already left the browser.
- A transcript, not an event. `checkout start` and `checkout ok` would have to be joined by hand, and they cannot be — neither line carries an identifier. Severity is also wrong: everything is a plain `log` or an `error` whether or not a human must act.
- The failure is swallowed. The `catch` logs and returns `failedOrder()`, so the exception never reaches the SDK's global handlers and error tracking has no issue for it: during an incident the searchable error list is empty while support holds the complaints.

Refactor, same contract (`Promise<Order>`, `failedOrder()` on failure):

```ts
import * as Sentry from "@sentry/browser";

export async function checkout(user: User, cart: Cart): Promise<Order> {
  const startedAt = performance.now();
  try {
    const order = await createOrder(user, cart);
    logger.info("checkout.completed", {
      order_id: order.id,              // identifier, not the order
      item_count: cart.items.length,
      account_tier: user.tier,         // low cardinality, not PII
      payment_method: order.payment.kind,
      duration_ms: Math.round(performance.now() - startedAt),
      outcome: "ok",
      release: import.meta.env.VITE_RELEASE,
      trace_id: activeTraceId(),       // read from the SDK's active span
    });
    return order;
  } catch (error) {
    logger.error("checkout.failed", {
      item_count: cart.items.length,
      account_tier: user.tier,
      duration_ms: Math.round(performance.now() - startedAt),
      outcome: "failed",
      release: import.meta.env.VITE_RELEASE,
      trace_id: activeTraceId(),
    });
    Sentry.captureException(error);    // no longer invisible to error tracking
    return failedOrder();
  }
}
```

What each change buys: stable fields (`outcome`, `duration_ms`) make the operation countable and aggregatable — failure rate, slow checkouts, and `order_id` as the jump from a support ticket to the exact event. One wide event at the boundary replaces three thin lines, so the start log disappears: the duration and timestamp carry its meaning. Severity now encodes a decision — `info` for a completed operation, `error` for one a human may need to look at. Identifiers replace payloads: no email, no cart contents, no order object. And `trace_id` plus `release` is what lets the event be read next to its trace, its error and its replay; without it the wide event is still an island.

Two things this code deliberately does not do. It does not decide retention or who can read the export — that is the data owner's policy, not the client logger's. And it does not log free-form user text: if `error.message` can carry any, redact at the SDK boundary (`beforeSend`-level), never rely on a downstream filter. Note also that `logger` here is the project's structured logger and `activeTraceId()` reads the active span: a hand-rolled logger does not get correlation for free.

code.code:
export async function checkout(cart: Cart): Promise<Order> {
  const span = tracer.startSpan("checkout");
  const order = await createOrder(cart);
  span.end();
  return order;
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
Two different bugs, one per path.

Failure path: `createOrder` rejects, the rejection propagates at the `await`, and `span.end()` is never reached. A span that is never ended is never reported: the batch exporter only ships finished spans, so the trace contains no `checkout` span at all — which is why failed checkouts 'cannot be found'. There is nothing to find, the failure is recorded on no span, and the open span object is a leak until garbage collection, not an extra export.

Success path: `startSpan` creates a span but does not make it active. The HTTP span created inside `createOrder` therefore has no current span to attach to and starts its own trace, which is the payment request appearing as a separate root. The checkout span itself does end, so it looks correct in isolation — that is why this half survived review even though it breaks the call tree on the happy path.

Rewrite:

```ts
import { SpanStatusCode } from "@opentelemetry/api";

export function checkout(cart: Cart): Promise<Order> {
  return tracer.startActiveSpan("checkout", async (span) => {
    try {
      return await createOrder(cart);       // children attach to this span
    } catch (error) {
      span.recordException(error);
      span.setStatus({ code: SpanStatusCode.ERROR });
      throw error;                          // callers still see the failure
    } finally {
      span.end();                           // success, rejection, sync throw
    }
  });
}
```

Why each piece matters. `startActiveSpan` makes the span current for the callback, so the outgoing request inside `createOrder` is parented to `checkout` and the tree survives the boundary — `startSpan` alone never does that. `finally` is the difference between 'no span' and 'a span with a duration'. `recordException` plus an error status is what lets the trace explain the failure; a span with only a duration tells you something was slow, not that it broke. And `return` the `startActiveSpan` call: `startActiveSpan` resolves to whatever the callback returns, and dropping the return silently turns this function into `Promise<void>` and breaks the `Promise<Order>` contract — the kind of regression that type-checks only because nobody looked.

Verify with two forced cases: a rejection must produce a single trace with `checkout` as the parent of the payment span and status ERROR; a success must still produce one trace, not two roots. Keep one stable operation name per operation — span-per-function produces a tree nobody can read.

